I wouldn't say that. Sound isn't light, and a bat's sound image processing is 
quite different from visual image processing.

Neither bats nor dolphins are optically blind, btw. "Blind as a bat" comes 
from the flight patterns of bats, which look as if they can't see where 
they're going, but they change course mid-flight to grab insects, which the 
batwatcher may not see. Blind animals in otherwise sighted taxa typically 
live in caves or underground. (Bats live in caves, but they fly outside 
them.)

